// Woolly Bugger: Russ Blessing's streamer, in Charlie Craven's modern dressing.

id woolly-bugger
name "Woolly Bugger"
type streamer
imitates baitfish attractor
naturals sculpin fry-minnow leech damselfly-nymph
difficulty beginner
species trout bass panfish sea-trout
season january february march april may june july august september october november december
origin originator="Russell Blessing" year=1967 region="Pennsylvania, USA"
description "A marabou tail, an olive chenille body, a black saddle hackle palmered along it and a copper wire rib."
history """
    Russ Blessing of Harrisburg, Pennsylvania, is believed to have tied the first Woolly Bugger
    in 1967, as a hellgrammite imitation with more movement. He took the marabou tail from Mark
    Sosin's Blossom fly and meant to trim the hackle, then left it as it was after seeing the
    fly in the water. Barry Beck's 1984 article in Fly Fisherman made it known everywhere.

    It descends from the Woolly Worm and the old British palmer flies.
    """
fishing """
    Blessing started with a dead drift near the bottom, and when that failed tried swings,
    jigging or strips. In lakes, work it back from along the shore, with a sinking-tip line in
    deep water. Brown ones pass for a crayfish, and where a stream has sculpins it can be fished
    like one; split shot or a weighted leader keeps it near the bottom.
    """
sources {
    "Wikipedia: Woolly Bugger" url="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woolly_Bugger"
    "Fly Fisherman: Imitative Woolly Buggers (Paul Marriner)" url="https://www.flyfisherman.com/editorial/imitative-woolly-buggers/514688"
    "Fly Fisherman: The Woolly Bugger, 1984 (Barry Beck)" url="https://www.flyfisherman.com/editorial/woolly-bugger/516095"
    "Fly Fisherman: Fly tying the Woolly Bugger (Charlie Craven)" url="https://www.flyfisherman.com/editorial/fly-tying-the-wooly-bugger/151947"
    "MidCurrent: Fishing Woolly Buggers (Gary Soucie)" url="https://midcurrent.com/flies/fishing-woolly-buggers/"
}
// Photos for checking proportions while authoring (pnpm refcheck); never published.
references {
    "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a9/Black_Woolly_Bugger.jpg" page="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_Woolly_Bugger.jpg" author="Mike Cline" license="CC BY-SA 4.0" note="Side view, flash in the tail"
    "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/81/Black_Woolly_Bugger_by_James_Stripes.jpg" page="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_Woolly_Bugger_by_James_Stripes.jpg" author="James Stripes" license="CC BY 2.5" note="Side view on a grid, bead head"
    "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/15/BlackandBrownBeadheadWoollyBugger.jpg" page="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BlackandBrownBeadheadWoollyBugger.jpg" author="Mike Cline" license="Public domain" note="Side view, bead head"
}

hook streamer-4xl size=8
thread black size="6/0"

materials {
    weight lead-free-wire
    tail marabou color=black
    flash krystal-flash count=4
    rib copper-wire
    body chenille color=olive
    // Assumption: hackle fibres about the gape, a little short of the body's depth.
    hackle saddle-hackle color=black fibre=1.0
}

steps {
    weight weight from=0.35 to=0.8 note="Lock the turns with thread dams at both ends."
    attach-thread at=0.85
    wrap-thread to=0.0
    // Craven: the tail reaches about one shank length past the bend.
    tie-in tail length=3.5 note="Bind the butts along the top of the shank for an even underbody."
    tie-in flash length=3.3 note="Two strands on each side."
    tie-in rib
    tie-in body note="Strip a little fluff to expose the core where it is tied in."
    wrap-thread to=0.9
    wind body to=0.9 turns=17 note="Touching turns, ending one eye-length behind the eye."
    tie-off body
    tie-in hackle note="By the butt, the inside of the feather facing back."
    wind hackle to=0.05 turns=8 note="Even open turns back to the bend."
    wind rib to=0.9 turns=7 note="Wiggle the wire so it traps no fibres."
    tie-off rib
    trim hackle
    whip-finish note="A small, smooth head."
}
